{"availability":"available","full":"Dyalog/Jarvis","name":"Jarvis","description":"APL-based web service framework supporting JSON or REST","category":"Developer tools","language":"APL","stars":41,"forks":15,"created":"2020-01-06T17:00:37Z","updated":"2026-10-09T16:26:29Z","license":"MIT","branch":"master","homepage":"https://dyalog.github.io/Jarvis/","topics":["apl","dyalog","dyalog-apl","http-server","json-server","rest-api","rest-server","web-services","webserver"],"demo":"https://dyalog.github.io/Jarvis/","color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-12T06:28:16.119Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-12T06:28:16.119Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-12T06:28:16.119Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-09T03:37:57.629Z","stars":40,"forks":15},{"at":"2026-10-12T06:28:16.119Z","stars":41,"forks":15}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Jarvis is Dyalog's web service framework, written in Dyalog APL. It makes your APL functions available over HTTP, so any client that can make an HTTP request can call your APL code. That includes a browser, a phone app, or a program written in Python, C#, JavaScript or APL.","The name is a pseudo-acronym for JSON and REST Service."],"features":["In the JSON paradigm, each APL function is an endpoint. Jarvis converts JSON to and from APL arrays for you.","In the REST paradigm, you write one function per HTTP method (Get, Post and so on) and decide how to handle each resource.","A built-in HTML interface lets you try your endpoints from a browser, with no client code.","Hooks for validation, authentication, session start-up and post-processing.","Sessions, HTTPS and client certificates, CORS and gzip/deflate compression."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Dyalog/Jarvis/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"0bbdfd6caef63f804c3c900e47de93258e6362d6bdbbe0007cd59e1851e31e36","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T03:37:57.795Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T03:37:57.795Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":